Date: Thu, 16 Sep 93 18:52:41 GMT
From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: Miscellaneous

Tim Starr:

> BTW, for Mike Price's benefit, it wasn't until I started reading
> t.p.g. that I made up my mind about being against victim disarmament.

Tim Starr seems to have formed the opinion that I regard him as
dogmatic, bigoted individual who forms opinions before he examines the
evidence.  Actually I have revised my opinion.  :-)

Tim goes on to say:

> I am persuaded by good arguments all the time.  If you haven't seen
> me persuaded of anything, it's because the arguments haven't been any
> good.  That is, not only do they fail as they are stated, but I can't
> even imagine how they could be formulated in such a way as to
> succeed.

The problem is that our methodologies and world-views are too disparate. 
Tim is a philosopher and, I'm afraid, suffers, as Perry so acutely
observed, from a common malaise of that profession - namely he tries to
understand the world in terms of woolly informal language.  Hard-core
Objectivists, by and large (and this is based on personal observation),
suffer from this.  Hence the obsession they have with their bibles (like
the Objectivist Lexicon).  Once they understand words, they feel, they
can understand the universe and they start blathering on about existence
exists and A is A and other such meaningless tautologies.  I shan't
bother to state that this is literally gibberish but, from the
perspective of having a background in the hard sciences, this all cuts
no ice with me.  The fundamental objects of enquiry in science are
defined in operational fashion and tied to meter readings, gauges etc. 
Can you formulate an operational test of "existence exists"?  No. 
Therefore it is a meaningless utterance.

I'm sure Tim does look at the evidence, it's just that our ways of
analysing problems (our respective methodologies) are so completely
different that very little common ground for communication exists. 
Incidently, this isn't just a dig at Objectivists, I have come to this
conclusion through extensive conversations with non-extropian, non-
objectivist philosophers and non-philosophers.  C.P. Snow's Two Cultures
divide.  More like a chasm, methinks.

> Tim Starr

