Guilty as charged. Although, I stopped purchasing imported Japanese
LaserDiscs several years ago. The exchange rate was just killer (around a
hundred bucks a disc). I'm glad to see many subjects being translated
(dubbed or subtitled) to english and released on video these days, for
near-reasonable prices. (Sigh, ahead of my time, again.)

Most, but not all, Anime has beautiful presentation, wonderful art and
animation.

I do, unfortunately, find a lot of entropic memes represented in Anime. The
Eco-Fascist and "things that man were not meant to know" are very
prevalent, not to mention the Japanese culture under-valueing individuality
and life. Even the two lefty mentioned, "Akira" has stupid scientists out
of control, creating psionic monsters that destroy Tokyo (twice, in the
same film). "Vampire Hunter-D" is post-apocalyptic. Both are great films.
Akira is even a GREAT film. 

Just be careful and don't let the bad memes bite.

My picks:

Second Lefty's Akira and Vampire Hunter-D (they aren't even close to as
Entropic as many other films).

Available in English:
Castle Cagliostro, Madox-01, Laputa (very eco-fascist tho), Bubblegum
Crisis (a series featuring Priss and the Replicants as a rock'n'roll band).

Yet to be translated:
Five Star Stories, Wings of Honnemise, Vampire Princess Miyu, Dirty Pair:
Project Eden, Urotsukidoji (bizarre monster-sex co-produced by Penthouse
featuring high school girls being raped and killed by demons from hell, a
TRULY great series), and the MASSIVELY entropic film Windaria.

Oh, and not really in the Anime genre, but I'll add Twilight of the
Cockroaches and Gunhed to my list.

Konban Wa, Ichiban-san, or as Kei and Yuri would say, "Yatta, Yatta, Yatta!!!"

